# Procedure

\nopagebreak

## 1. RNA extraction and storage

\noindent\fbox{%
    \parbox{\textwidth}{%
        \textcolor{red}{Critical}: RNA is very unstable when isolated. As soon as it is pure in suspension, it is to be put on ice. RNA is stored in ${\neg}80{^\circ}C$. When working around RNA (on the RNA bench), filtered tips for pipettes are used, and everything should be nuclease-free (e.g. DEPC-treated water, nuclease-free eppendorf tubes, etc.).
    }%
}


## 2. cDNA synthesis and storage

By using a reverse transcriptase, RNA from any extraction is converted to cDNA and semi-amplified using a number of PCR cycles.

The protocol is from [qScript cDNA Synthesis Kit (Quantabio)](https://www.quantabio.com/media/contenttype/IFU-024.1_REV_02_95047_qScript_cDNA_Synthesis_Kit.pdf).


### 2.0 Required Materials

```{r RequiredMaterial}
#look up the names from the csv
usedEquipments <- c("Pipettor",
                    "Filtered Tips", 
                    "PCR machine (thermal cycler)") 

usedReagents <- c("qScript Reaction Mix (5X)", "Nuclease-free water")

usedBE <- c("RNA extract, 500 ng x tube",
          "qScript Reverse Transcriptase (20X), 1 pack")

usedContainers <- c("Eppendorf Tube", "PCR array tubes (0.2 mL each)")
```


```{r usedMaterials}
matTable <- usedMaterialsTable(usedEquipments, usedReagents,usedBE, usedContainers)
knitr::kable(matTable)
```

### 2.1. cDNA synthesis

1. Thaw all frozen components, place all of them on ice.

2. Add the following to a $0.2 mL$ PCR array tube sitting on ice for a final volume of $20 {\mu}L$:

    * RNA ($500 ng$), variable volume $-$ depends on RNA solution concentration

    * Nuclease-free water, variable volume

    * qScript Reaction Mix (5X), $4{\mu}L$

    * qScript RT, $1{\mu}L$

3. Vortex gently, then centrifuge for 10 seconds (PCR-tube centrifuge).

4. Place PCR array tubes in a thermal cycler with the following program:

      1 cycle: $22{^\circ}C$, 5 min
  
      1 cycle: $42{^\circ}C$, 30 min
  
      1 cycle: $85{^\circ}C$, 5 min

      $4{^\circ}C$ hold

5. (Optional) After completion of cDNA synthesis, use $1/5^{th}$ to $1/10^{th}$ of the first-strand reaction ($2-4{\mu}L$) for PCR amplification. If desired, cDNA product can be diluted with 10 mM Tris-HCl (pH 8.0), 0.1 mM EDTA and stored at ${\neg}20{^\circ}C$. 

