## 3. Primers

### 3.1 Primer design

Primer design is computationally done through the online web-tool [NCBI Primer-BLAST](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/tools/primer-blast/index.cgi). The designed primers will have a desired $T_{m}$, GC content, and specificity for the gene of interest. Because of isoforms and SNPs and genetic sequence variability in databases, [NCBI Gene](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene) is generally used as the reference gene database. From here, the desired gene is selected, and the transcript page is sought. On the transcript page, under "Analysis", "Design Primers" is selected and primers can be designed with desired parameters ($e.g.$ on exon-exon junctions).

### 3.2 Ordering primers

From Sigma-Aldrich, primer sequences are ordered, designed from NCBI Primer Design. The primers are named, and the names should include the targeted gene, organism if relevant, and other information to distinguish gene, purpose or characteristics ($e.g.$ CCR2ab, used to detect isoforms A and B of CCR2; while CCR5ab cannot detect the isoform C of CCR5). The primer sequences are put in from $5' - 3'$ direction ($i.e.$ from last nucleotide to first in the antisense primer). Other options are:

* 0.025 mole

* Desalt

* Dry

### 3.3 Primer stock preparation

Primers arrive with a datasheet specifying their properties ($e.g.$ $T_{m}$, 2ndry tendency, GC%). The datasheet also contains required volume of DEPC-treated water to reach $100 {\mu}M$ concentration. To prepare primer stocks, for each tube add double the respective specified volume to achieve a final concentration of $50 {\mu}M$. Store the primer in ${\neg}20C^{\circ}$ with other DNA. Primer stocks are used $1{\mu}L$ for each PCR reaction.

\noindent\fbox{%
    \parbox{\textwidth}{%
        \textcolor{orange}{Important}: Keep the datasheet. It contains important information for later use.
    }%
}

