## 4. PCR

### 4.0 Required Materials

```{r RequiredMaterialPCR}

usedEquipments <- c("Pipettor",
                    "Filtered Tips", 
                    "PCR machine (thermal cycler)") 

usedReagents <- c("qScript Reaction Mix (5X)", "Nuclease-free water")

usedBE <- c("RNA extract, 500 ng x tube",
          "qScript Reverse Transcriptase (20X), 1 pack")

usedContainers <- c("Eppendorf Tube", "PCR array tubes (0.2 mL each)")
```


```{r usedMaterialsPCR}
matTable <- usedMaterialsTable(usedEquipments, usedReagents,usedBE, usedContainers)
knitr::kable(matTable)
```

### 4.1 Preparing PCR tubes

\noindent\fbox{%
    \parbox{\textwidth}{%
        \textcolor{orange}{Important}: Before starting, each PCR tube should be named according to the primers, cell type, date etc.
    }%
}

Add the following to each $0.2 mL$ PCR tubes for a final volume of $20{\mu}L$:

  * PCR mix: $10{\mu}L$

  * cDNA: $2{\mu}L$
        
      This can be increased if the detection is poor (or the targeted RNA is sparsely transcribed)

  * Forward primer: $1{\mu}L$
    
      Leave out if negative control.
    
  * Reverse primer: $1{\mu}L$

      Leave out if negative control.
        
  * DEPC-treated water: $6-8{\mu}L$
    
      Fill until total volume $20{\mu}L$
    
### 4.2 Running PCR in a thermal cycler

For semi-quantitative PCR, the desired program is designed, and the tubes are placed in the thermal cycler.

\noindent\fbox{%
    \parbox{\textwidth}{%
        \textcolor{red}{Critical}: PCR tubes, when placed in the thermal cycler, should be closed air-tight. Otherwise, during the cycles, the PCR solution will evaporate and the experiment will fail (at least in the open tube).
    }%
}

The thermal cycler has 4 total steps, for a total duration of [01:16:00-01:36:00]:

1. Initialization ($95{^\circ}C$; 00:01:00)

2. PCR reaction; 35-45 cycles (01:10:00 - 01:30:00)

    2a. Denaturation ($95{^\circ}C$; 00:00:30)
    
        
    
    2b. Priming ($60-70{^\circ}C$; 00:01:00)
    
      This temperature is variable, it should be lower than the minimum $T_{m}$ of all of the primers.
    
    2c. Elongation ($72{^\circ}C$; 00:00:30 - 00:02:00)
    
      Optimal temperature for the polymerase enzyme to use the annealed primers to elongate the chain. Elongation step can be longer (e.g. 2 minutes) if the expected PCR product is longer (e.g. 2000 bp). 30 seconds is good for products less than 1000 bp.
    
3. Wrap-up polymerase reaction ($72{^\circ}C$ - 00:05:00)

4. Hold ($4{^\circ}C$)

